Title: Link winwrap into the standalone node.exe for Windows XP
Origin: electron-legacy (this repo)

Only reached when building Node on its own. Electron compiles the same libuv,
V8 and src/ through GN and never reads node.gyp, so this is inert there -- the
same arrangement as 04-node-version-check-winxp.patch.

Three settings, all on the node executable rather than on node_lib or on every
target in the tree. Not on cctest or the embedtests either: those run on the
build machine, and giving them the /INCLUDE: without the library is LNK2001.

1. The dependency, so winwrap is built and its library is available.

2. AdditionalDependencies+, which prepends winwrap.lib to the link line. The
   '+' is the whole point: a plain AdditionalDependencies appends, which puts
   winwrap after the import libraries already there.

   libuv names advapi32 explicitly, in deps/uv/uv.gyp's link_settings, so
   advapi32.lib is a command-line library and position decides between it and
   winwrap. kernel32 arrives only through the /DEFAULTLIB directives the SDK
   headers embed, and those are processed after everything named on the line.
   That is why the kernel32 half of winwrap's table needs no help and the
   advapi32 half does -- and why the symptom named advapi32 functions only:

     lld-link : error : __declspec(dllimport) _EventWriteTransfer@28 was replaced
     lld-link : error : __declspec(dllimport) _RegGetValueW@28 was replaced

3. /INCLUDE: on winwrap's anchor, and MinimumRequiredVersion.

Steps 2 and 3 are not alternatives, which cost a round to learn. winwrap's
docs/USAGE.md is explicit: "Ordering decides who wins a reference that
exists; the anchor guarantees the redirect object is in the link even for a
slot nothing in the binary happens to call." winwrap.gyp's own header lists
only the anchor and the subsystem version as the consumer's work, and is
incomplete.

winwrap is fetched into deps/winwrap by build_node.sh, and the define its
.asm forwarders guard on needs 12-gyp-masm-settings.patch to reach ml.

Note for anyone editing this: the OS=="win" entry is merged into the node
target's existing 'conditions' list, not given a 'conditions' key of its own.
A .gyp file is a Python literal, so two keys of the same name do not merge --
the last silently wins and the first is discarded. The first version of this
patch did that, and the result parsed cleanly, generated cleanly, and produced
a node.exe with /INCLUDE:_winwrap_static_redirects on the link line and no
winwrap anywhere in the build. ast.literal_eval cannot catch it, because
duplicate keys are legal Python.

diff --git a/node.gyp b/node.gyp
--- a/node.gyp
+++ b/node.gyp
@@ -594,6 +594,48 @@
 
       'msvs_settings': {
         'VCLinkerTool': {
+          # Prepended, and the '+' is what does it: gyp appends a plain
+          # AdditionalDependencies, which would put winwrap after the import
+          # libraries already on this line and lose every reference that
+          # resolves before winwrap is reached.
+          #
+          # It matters here because libuv names advapi32 explicitly, in
+          # deps/uv/uv.gyp's link_settings, so advapi32.lib is a command-line
+          # library and position decides between it and winwrap. kernel32
+          # arrives only through the /DEFAULTLIB directives the SDK headers
+          # embed, and those are processed last, which is why the kernel32
+          # half of winwrap's table needs nothing and the advapi32 half does:
+          #
+          #   lld-link : error : __declspec(dllimport) _RegGetValueW@28
+          #                      was replaced
+          #
+          # The /INCLUDE: below does not cover this. The anchor guarantees the
+          # redirect object is in the link; ordering decides who wins a
+          # reference that already exists. winwrap's docs/USAGE.md calls for
+          # both, and both are needed.
+          'AdditionalDependencies+': ['$(OutDir)lib\\winwrap.lib'],
+          'conditions': [
+            # The subsystem version is the other half of running on XP:
+            # winwrap supplies the imports, this is what lets XP's loader
+            # accept the binary at all. 5.01 for Win32 and 5.02 for x64,
+            # as low as the linker goes for each machine.
+            #
+            # /INCLUDE: names winwrap's anchor, forcing the redirect table
+            # into the link even for slots nothing in the binary calls. The
+            # symbol takes the usual decoration, hence the underscore on x86.
+            ['target_arch=="ia32"', {
+              'MinimumRequiredVersion': '5.01',
+              'AdditionalOptions': [
+                '/INCLUDE:_winwrap_static_redirects',
+              ],
+            }],
+            ['target_arch=="x64"', {
+              'MinimumRequiredVersion': '5.02',
+              'AdditionalOptions': [
+                '/INCLUDE:winwrap_static_redirects',
+              ],
+            }],
+          ],
           'GenerateMapFile': 'true', # /MAP
           'MapExports': 'true', # /MAPINFO:EXPORTS
           'RandomizedBaseAddress': 2, # enable ASLR
@@ -613,6 +655,23 @@
       'msvs_disabled_warnings!': [4244],
 
       'conditions': [
+        # winwrap supplies the Win32 functions XP does not export, by defining
+        # the __imp_<name> slots the call sites load through. It belongs on the
+        # executable rather than on node_lib: the slots are resolved at the
+        # final link, and that is the link the import libraries are also on.
+        #
+        # Merged into this list rather than given a 'conditions' key of its own.
+        # A .gyp file is a Python literal, so a second key of the same name does
+        # not merge -- the last one silently wins and the first is discarded,
+        # which parses perfectly and builds a node.exe with no winwrap in it.
+        #
+        # Only this target. cctest and the embedtests run on the build machine,
+        # and giving them the /INCLUDE: below without the library is LNK2001.
+        [ 'OS=="win"', {
+          'dependencies': [
+            'deps/winwrap/winwrap.gyp:winwrap',
+          ],
+        }],
         [ 'node_shared_hdr_histogram=="false"', {
           'dependencies': [
             'deps/histogram/histogram.gyp:histogram',
